<title>USA: Maine faces another vote on clear-cutting pact.</title>
<headline>Maine faces another vote on clear-cutting pact.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1996-11-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>A proposal by Maine Gov. Angus King that would limit the clear-cutting of forests must go before the voters again because it failed to capture at least 50 percent of the votes that were cast.</p>
<p>The more radical proposal, which would entirely have banned the forestry practice used by the paper industry, got less than one-third of the vote. And opponents to both of the clear-cutting proposals got around one-fifth of the vote.</p>
<p>The supporters of the total ban said the battle is far from over and they plan to fight the governor's proposal when it goes before the voters again. A final date for the new vote has not yet been set.</p>
<p>--U.S. Municipal Desk, 212-859-1650</p>
</text>
